FINAL ANSWER:
Status: I've finished the blame analysis and am writing it up.

**Short answer:** Live Go code is not concentrated in a few people, so losing one or two would not hurt. The bigger issue is that the top authors left years ago, so nobody active knows most of the code.

**How I measured:** I ran `git blame` on every tracked `.go` file at HEAD. I ignored whitespace changes and the `interface{}` to `any` commit (6bf5b04). I excluded `vendor/`, `_test.go` files and the generated `zz_generated_flags.go`. That leaves 6,397 lines from 73 authors.

| Author | Live lines | Share | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|
| Eric Paris | 1042 | 16.3% | 2018-10 |
| Riobard | 922 | 14.4% | 2014-03 |
| Noah Dietz | 520 | 8.1% | 2018-11 |
| Jonathan Reyna | 420 | 6.6% | 2017-01 |
| therealmitchconnors | 394 | 6.2% | 2019-09 |
| Alex Ogier | 383 | 6.0% | 2015-02 |
| Tamal Saha | 308 | 4.8% | 2018-08 |

- **Concentration:** The top 1 author owns 16.3%, the top 2 own 30.7%, and the top 7 own 62.4%. The other 66 authors share the remaining 37.6%.
- **Ownership by file:** The big files are mostly Alex Ogier's (`flag.go`, 1,292 lines). Each flag type has its own owner:
  - Slice types: Reyna (bool, uint, ip), Dietz (int32, int64, float32, float64) and Paris (string, int).
  - Map types: Saha (`string_to_string.go`, `string_to_int.go`).
  - Other files: Besogonov (`string_to_int64.go`), Ethan P. (`errors.go`) and Lena Voss (`flagset_helpers.go`).
  - These are small, self-contained files.
- **Activity:** None of the top 7 have committed since 2019. Since 2024 there have been 46 non-merge commits, spread thinly. Tomas Aschan has the most with 9, and nobody else has more than 4. The latest commit is 2026-04-08.

**What this means for review:**
- No single departure removes much knowledge, but the maintainers who wrote most of the core code are already gone. The code is effectively orphaned. Nobody active will remember why `flag.go` or the older scalar types work the way they do.
- Use `git log` and `git blame` on the file under review to find the original reasoning. `/project-pilot:history` can help with that.
- Most of the recent contributors listed above could review the leaf files, but I haven't checked what they changed.

**Caveats:**
- I did not apply a `.mailmap`. Some people appear under two names. For example, `bogem` and Albert Nigmatzianov are the same person, as are `mitchconnors` and `therealmitchconnors`. Their real share is somewhat higher than shown. This doesn't change the conclusion.
- Blame counts surviving lines, not effort or review quality.
- I did not compute each author's share within a single file. For example, I know Ogier is the top author of `flag.go`, but not by what margin.
- The generated file (488 lines) is attributed to one commit. It is derived output, so I left it out.

