FINAL ANSWER:
**Bus factor:** 5 people cover over 50% of surviving Go code. That number is misleading, because almost all of those people have left.

I used `git blame` (`-w -M -C`) on HEAD instead of the skill's commit counts. Blame counts lines that still exist, which is what you asked about. I skipped `vendor/` and `zz_generated_flags.go`. That leaves 75 Go files and 12,194 live lines from 77 authors. I did not launch the contributor-analyzer agent. I ran the git commands directly.

**Who owns the live code** (last commit in parentheses):

| Author | Lines | Share | Cumulative | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Eric Paris | 2020 | 16.6% | 16.6% | 2018 |
| Alex Ogier | 1237 | 10.1% | 26.7% | 2015 |
| Noah Dietz | 1228 | 10.1% | 36.8% | 2018 |
| Jonathan Reyna | 1044 | 8.6% | 45.3% | 2017 |
| Tamal Saha | 624 | 5.1% | 50.5% | 2018 |
| Riobard | 538 | 4.4% | 54.9% | 2014 |
| Mitch Connors | 522 | 4.3% | 59.2% | 2019 |
| Georges Varouchas | 454 | 3.7% | 62.9% | 2025-06 |

**Active people:** 12 people committed in the last 12 months, excluding dependabot. Together they own 1,229 lines, about 10% of the code.
- **Largest active owners:** Georges Varouchas (454), Maximilian Frank (172), Hu Jun (136), Lena Voss (133), Patrick Ohly (108).
- **Latest commits:** Sam Okafor (2026-04) and Lena Voss (2026-03) are the most recent. Both own few lines.
- **Tomas Aschan:** 32 commits in 2 years but only 67 live lines.

**Would losing one or two people be a problem?** No. The top 4 owners hold 45% of the code, but all four are long gone, and the library works without them. The real risk is that nobody active knows most of the code.
- **Knowledge gap:** about 90% of the code has no active author. The biggest active owner holds 3.7%.
- **Single-owner files:** 40 of 75 files have one person above 80% of lines. 11 are 100% one person.
- **Core file:** `flag.go` has 1,292 lines. Ogier owns 33%, Paris 14% and Nigmatzianov 9%. All three are inactive since 2015–2018.
- **Slice types** (`*_slice.go`): mostly Reyna, Dietz and Volosatov. All inactive since 2017–2018. Each file is generic and repetitive, so the risk is low.
- **`string_to_string.go`:** 94% Tamal Saha (inactive since 2018).
- **`bytes.go`:** 59% Eugene Agafonov (inactive since 2018).
- **`flagset_helpers.go`:** Lena Voss owns 133 lines. This is the largest recent single-owner area and it is still active. Review it.

**What this means for your PR reviews:**
- You are probably the main person who knows the core. Read `flag.go`'s history (`git log -p` and blame) before approving changes to parsing or `FlagSet` behavior.
- Ask Varouchas, Frank, Hu Jun or Voss for a second opinion on PRs that touch their files.
- Rely on tests for the slice types and the other repetitive files. They are low risk.

**Caveats:**
- Blame counts line authorship. It does not measure understanding. A line moved or reformatted by someone else may be attributed to that person.
- I did not check which files other files import, so I did not rank files by importance. I used file size and the `flag.go` role instead.
- "Active" means a commit in the last 12 months. I did not check PR review activity or comments, which the git history does not show.
- The `example.net` emails for Voss and Okafor look synthetic. I took them at face value.

