FINAL ANSWER:
**Short answer:** most of the surviving Go code was written by people who have left. The part still written by active people is spread thin, with a few people carrying most of it.

I counted surviving lines with `git blame` on the 76 non-vendored `.go` files (12,682 lines). Rename and move tracking was on, and whitespace was ignored. "Active" means a commit in the 12 months before the newest commit (2026-04-08).

**Top authors of surviving lines**

| Author | Lines | Share | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| Eric Paris | 2020 | 15.9% | inactive, last commit 2018 |
| Alex Ogier | 1237 | 9.8% | inactive, 2015 |
| Noah Dietz | 1228 | 9.7% | inactive, 2018 |
| JReyLBC | 1044 | 8.2% | not in the active list |
| Tamal Saha | 624 | 4.9% | inactive, 2018 |
| riobard | 538 | 4.2% | not in the active list |
| **Sam Okafor** | 537 | 4.2% | active |
| Mitch Connors | 522 | 4.1% | inactive, 2019 |
| **Georges Varouchas** | 454 | 3.6% | active |

**Concentration**
- **Active authors:** 14 people account for 2,012 lines, which is **15.9%** of the Go code. The other 84% belongs to authors who are not active.
- **Among active authors:** Sam Okafor, Georges Varouchas and Eth-p (295 lines) hold 1,286 of the 2,012 lines, about 64%. Nobody else active has more than 172 lines.
- **Recent commit weighting:** the busfactor script's recency-weighted score gives Sam Okafor about 51% of the whole repo. The next two are Tomas Aschan (about 9%) and Georges Varouchas (about 9%). The script marks the repo root as bus factor 1.
- **`.github/`:** the script flags this as a separate risk. 72% of its weight comes from Mark Sagi-Kazar, who is inactive, and Tomas Aschan is the only active author left there.

**Would losing one or two people be a problem?**
- **Losing Sam Okafor:** yes. Sam is the largest active author by both measures. Sam's recent commits also make up most of the recency-weighted score.
- **Losing Sam and Georges:** this would remove about half of the Go lines that active people hold. Reviews would then fall to people with under 175 lines each.
- **The bigger problem:** it already exists. The original authors of the core flag-parsing code are gone, so no active reviewer wrote most of it. Reviewing PRs there means reading the code fresh, not relying on a past author.

**Caveats**
- **Generated code:** `zz_generated_flags.go` is included in the counts. Whoever last regenerated it is credited with its lines, so some of Sam's and Georges's totals may be generator output and not hand-written code. I didn't separate it out.
- **Inactive status:** I classed JReyLBC and riobard as not active because they have no commit in the window. I didn't check their histories. Anyone who reviews without committing would look like a stranger in these numbers.
- **Repo history:** it has 255 commits since 2012 and is not an import or a young repo. So the low active share reflects real turnover.
- **GitHub logins:** I ran the script with `--no-github`, so owners appear as emails. Some emails may belong to the same person, which would make the active concentration look lower than it is.

I ran the script on a throwaway clone under `<tmp> so nothing was written to your repo. I haven't generated the HTML report or a CODEOWNERS file. I can do either if you want.
